WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:07.399
It's been about three months since this, what
I call my $829 10 minute doctor's visit.

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:11.480
I told the initial part of the story before
and I've been getting emails from people,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.940
including podiatrists saying, David, what
ended up happening with this?

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.430
So here's the backstory.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:22.920
In early June, I went to see a podiatrist
because I was having some foot and a knee

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.810
pain.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.820
And uh, my regular doctor suggested I go,
I did all the right things.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.920
I called my insurance.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.449
I said, is podiatry covered?

00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:32.449
They said, yes, it is.

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:33.770
I said, do I need a referral?

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:36.430
They said, no, you don't do I need prior?

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:37.930
Do I need pre-authorization?

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:42.850
They said, no, you don't just book it and
go, you'll pay your $40 copay or whatever

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.070
it is for specialist and you're going to be
good to go.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:49.160
And I went and uh, the doctor said, hey, uh,
okay.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.610
I think that the problem you're having is
that because of, uh, your flat arches, it's

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.720
throwing your entire ankles and knees out
of alignment.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.670
You should get some or thought kind of custom
orthotic insoles, which of course they didn't

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.570
make their, nor are they covered by insurance.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.580
The orthotics are 350 or 375 bucks.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.259
Here's a prescription, but a boom, better
bang, you're outta here.

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:11.259
Okay, I pay my copay.

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:16.310
And then a few weeks later I get a bill, a
bill saying your visit with the specialist,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:21.270
which was 10 minutes, maybe it was 10 minutes,
it was 10 minutes with me spending three minutes

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.439
sitting there while of the podiatrist types
up notes.

00:01:24.439 --> 00:01:25.439
Right?

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:28.229
That was a 460 or so dollar visit.

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:31.429
You've paid your $40 copay, it's not covered.

00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:34.580
So you owe the other $420 or so.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.329
And I called the insurance and I want to tell
a shorter version of this since I've already

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:39.329
told this.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:45.159
I called my insurance and they say, well,
podiatry is covered and you don't need a referral

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:50.939
and you don't need prior authorization, but
your diagnosis was past planning or as we

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:53.299
would say in English flat feet.

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:58.670
And that is not a diagnosis that is covered
to which I said, wait a second, how I'm not

00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:59.670
a doctor.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.670
Do you agree?

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:01.670
I'm not a doctor?

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.389
Yes, yes, we agree you're not a doctor.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:09.460
Um, how would I know whether to expect a diagnosis
which would not be covered?

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.840
It doesn't really seem to make any sense.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.430
So in the end, what was potentially going
to happen is I was going to owe for this 10

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:22.280
minute visit, which results in me getting
some orthotics, um, 350 or three 75 for the

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.900
orthotics and four 40 or so, whatever.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.040
For the a specialist visit, it was going to
be roughly $830.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.180
Now I've got a bunch of emails from podiatrist
saying, David, I, I deal with this with patients.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:40.280
And it's crazy that on the basis of a diagnosis
code, you would either be covered or not covered,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.900
but here are a bunch of other codes that,
this is not insurance fraud, but these are

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:53.100
just other ways that you can report that someone
came in with a, a, a pain related to a foot

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.100
condition.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.020
And here's how I would often code it.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.160
So I played the game.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:02.070
Now at a certain point, I mean, listen, the
truth is if this ends up costing me eight

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:06.350
29, I'm fortunate enough to be in a situation
where it's not going to bankrupt me and I'm

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:10.390
putting a lot of time into this, but I do
want to see it through just to see like how

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.030
long will it take to ultimately navigate this
and what will be the result.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.290
So I got in touch with the, uh, specialist.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:22.620
They put me in touch with some billing person
who of course, should they even exist in a

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.880
system that makes sense.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.890
Is that even a position that we should have
inflating the bureaucratic cost of these visits?

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.120
I would say no.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.790
They said we're going to resubmit it.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.360
I'm going to talk to the doctor when the doctor,
if the doctor agrees to resubmit it in a different

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.040
way, we will do that.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.170
Call us in a month.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:45.630
I called in a month and was told that that
billing person had left and not on good terms,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.010
therefore no one there knows what it is that
they were working on.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:54.570
So we again started from scratch and I am
here to report to you today that there has

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.300
been no progress whatsoever.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:01.780
My, uh, the, the specialist has said, we're
not going to start charging you late fees.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.900
We understand that we're in the process of
trying to work this out so you're not going

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.580
to owe us late fees.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:12.740
But it has finally been resubmitted to my
insurance with some other diagnostic codes.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:17.319
Uh, and I'm waiting to hear back and it could
be several more weeks before they either accept

00:04:17.319 --> 00:04:23.960
or deny the claim, the consider all of the
people and for what was seven minutes of clinical

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.949
time and three minutes of notetaking time.

00:04:26.949 --> 00:04:32.789
Consider not only what I might end up paying,
but the amount of hours that I've put into

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:33.789
this.

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:36.719
Again, partially for you to have stuff to
talk about on the show because of the curiosity

00:04:36.719 --> 00:04:39.830
of the entire thing, but this has been hours
of my time.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.400
Add up all of the hours of everybody else
who's been involved.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.009
There's people at my insurance company that
have to process this claim two times multiple

00:04:48.009 --> 00:04:54.099
people at the specialist's office, including
the podiatrist himself working on resubmitting

00:04:54.099 --> 00:04:55.530
this, I'm calling.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:02.379
It's insane the system that we have and in
the end, I'm not really any closer to avoiding

00:05:02.379 --> 00:05:07.270
an $829 10 minute doctor's visit than I was
when this all started.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:12.979
Now if you're going to email me and say, David,
in my country, a custom orthotic insoles are

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.350
only a hundred bucks.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.280
I already heard from two dozen of you.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.280
I understand that.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:23.820
I, I trust me, I get that even the price of
$350 is a crazy price and people from Canada

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.199
and the UK saying, I just got custom ones
for 80 bucks, a hundred bucks and the insurance

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:29.419
even covers it.

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:32.879
In some cases they don't even depress me further
with those stories.

00:05:32.879 --> 00:05:37.970
I get that that piece is going on, but that's
the latest and I will certainly keep you abreast

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:37.990
of any developments there.

